    "Projects to watch: The October announcement named a DP World memorandum of understanding with Arctic Gateway Group and CentrePort Canada relating to a possible Churchill–Hudson Bay logistics corridor, a Masdar–Natural Resources Canada energy-cooperation arrangement and an investor-networking agreement. These are concrete cooperation milestones; project expenditure and operational results require separate verification.",
    "A longer history than the latest U.S. tariffs: Canada's UAE relationship predates the most recent tariff friction with Washington. The two countries began investment-treaty negotiations in 2016, and an investment-protection agreement entered into force in May 2026. It would be misleading to attribute every future Canada–UAE transaction to U.S. trade policy.",
    "Who benefits—and how to tell: Canada may gain new customers and bargaining flexibility; the UAE may gain investment opportunities and closer access to North American supply chains. U.S. exporters might face more competition in some categories, but the agreement itself is not proof of an American economic loss. The measurable test is post-implementation export growth, actual investment outlays, completed projects and changes in U.S. supplier market share against comparable markets."
